/* ==============================================================
   04 — Posture configuration: two ways to reach one pose
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   SetConf, SetAutoConf — lefty and righty
   NEEDS     Robot activated, plenty of clear space around the arm
   WATCH     The tool tip stays put while the elbow swaps sides
   TIME      ~35 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE THING NOBODY MENTIONS UNTIL IT BITES

   A pose says where the tool tip is and how it is turned. Four
   numbers. But a two-link arm can usually reach the same point
   in TWO different ways: with the elbow bent one way, or bent
   the other. Mirror images of each other, about the line from
   the base to the tool.

   Same tool tip. Same orientation. Completely different-looking
   robot — and the elbow is on the opposite side of the arm.

   One parameter says which:

       SetConf(ce)

     ce =  1   "righty"   joint 2 positive
     ce = -1   "lefty"    joint 2 negative

   (The names come from human arms. Look down on your own right
   arm reaching forward across a desk: the elbow sits out to the
   right, and the forearm angles back in. That is righty.)

   Two options, one parameter. The six-axis Meca500 needs three
   parameters and has up to eight postures; the SCARA's whole
   story is two.

   WHY YOU SHOULD CARE

   The two postures are geometrically equivalent and practically
   very different:

     - the ELBOW is on opposite sides. In a cell with a wall, a
       feeder or a person on one side, one posture swings the
       elbow into it and the other does not
     - one may be far from a joint limit and the other close to
       it, or out of reach altogether
     - and if the robot silently changes posture between two
       cycles, the arm swings through a large, unexpected motion
       to get there — the classic "it moved somewhere strange"
       report

   NOT EVERY POINT HAS TWO

   In front of the robot, nearly every reachable point can be
   reached both ways. Behind the base, out to each side, it is
   different: joint 1 runs out of range for one of the two, so
   one side can only be reached lefty and the other only righty.
   The user manual's workspace drawing shows the zones; the robot
   will tell you too, by refusing.

   BY DEFAULT: AUTOMATIC

   Automatic posture selection is on by default. The robot picks
   the posture that is fastest to reach from where it currently
   is, which keeps motion small.

       SetAutoConf(1)    robot chooses (default)
       SetAutoConf(0)    freeze the current posture
       SetConf(ce)       demand a specific posture
                         (this also switches automatic off)

   THE RULE OF THUMB

   In a real cell, pin the configuration with SetConf at the
   start of the program. The manual goes further than most
   manuals do: it strongly recommends always specifying it, and
   warns that automatic selection may pick differently after a
   small change in a target — or after a firmware update that
   improves the selection algorithm. It costs one line and it
   removes an entire category of intermittent, hard-to-reproduce
   faults — the ones where a cell runs for three weeks and then
   one cycle takes the long way round.

   MoveLin has a stricter rule: a linear move holds its posture
   for the whole path and CANNOT change it — physically, not as a
   policy. If a MoveLin is refused for no obvious reason, the
   start and end postures disagreeing is a prime suspect.

   THIS EXAMPLE MOVES A LOT

   Switching posture swings the elbow from one side to the other,
   straightening the arm on the way. Give the robot room and run
   it slowly the first time.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(15)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Automatic: the default ------------------------------------
   The robot reaches this pose whichever way is fastest from
   where it already is. Starting from the straight arm, either
   way is equally short, so which one you get is the
   controller's call — which is the behaviour we are about to
   remove. */
SetAutoConf(1)
MovePose(150, 0, 80, 0)
Delay(2)


/* --- The same pose, demanded both ways -------------------------
   Every MovePose line below is IDENTICAL. Only the SetConf above
   it changes. The tool tip goes to the same point in space every
   time, and the elbow swaps sides.

   This is the demo. Cover the code, let them watch the arm
   rearrange itself, and then reveal that the motion command
   never changed. */

SetConf(1)                  // righty
MovePose(150, 0, 80, 0)
Delay(2)

SetConf(-1)                 // lefty
MovePose(150, 0, 80, 0)
Delay(2)

SetConf(1)                  // righty again
MovePose(150, 0, 80, 0)
Delay(2)


/* --- Pinning it for a real program -----------------------------
   This is the pattern to actually use. Choose the posture once,
   at the top, and every pose below is reached the same way,
   every cycle, forever. */

SetConf(1)

MovePose(170, -50, 80, 0)
MovePose(170, 50, 80, 0)
MovePose(190, 0, 90, 0)


// --- Hand control back and go home ----------------------------
SetAutoConf(1)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. The lefty-righty swap is one of the best visual demos the
      robot has. It needs no fixture and no tool, and it explains
      inverse kinematics without the word being said.

   2. Ask an integrator whether their current robot ever "takes a
      strange route" between two taught points. If yes, this is
      usually the reason, and SetConf is the fix.

   3. Jog the arm behind the base, out to one side, and try
      MovePose to that point under SetConf(1) and then
      SetConf(-1). One works and one is refused — before
      anything moves. Demanding a posture the pose cannot be
      reached in produces an error and a stationary robot, not a
      half-finished move.

   NEXT   05_turn_configuration.mxprog — the same idea for the
          turning shaft, and the one that saves cables.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
